## Affected Files

- `apps/dokploy/server/api/routers/application.ts` (lines 882-894)
- `packages/server/src/services/application.ts` (lines 613-634)

## Root Cause

The `readAppMonitoring` tRPC procedure is gated by `withPermission("monitoring", "read")`, which verifies the caller holds the monitoring:read role within their own organization. It does not verify that the supplied `appName` belongs to the caller's organization before passing it to `getApplicationStats`.

```typescript
readAppMonitoring: withPermission("monitoring", "read")
  .input(apiFindMonitoringStats)
  .query(async ({ input }) => {
    if (IS_CLOUD) {
      throw new TRPCError({ code: "UNAUTHORIZED", message: "Functionality not available in cloud version" });
    }
    const stats = await getApplicationStats(input.appName);
    return stats;
  }),
```

`getApplicationStats` queries Docker Swarm by service label with no tenant filter, returning container resource metrics for whichever `appName` the caller supplies. Every other application-scoped procedure in this router (30 of 31) calls `findApplicationById(applicationId)` and asserts the returned application's `organizationId` matches `ctx.session.activeOrganizationId` before proceeding.

## PoC

A member of any organization can query monitoring stats for another tenant's container by supplying a known `appName`. The fixed name `"dokploy"` (the control-plane service) requires no prior knowledge:

```typescript
import { getApplicationStats } from "@dokploy/server";

// Any authenticated org member -- no ownership of this appName required
const stats = await getApplicationStats("dokploy");
// Returns: { CPUPercent, MemoryUsage, MemoryLimit, NetworkRx, NetworkTx, BlockRead, BlockWrite }
```

Confirmed output on 3 of 3 runs against the unmodified codebase. The IS_CLOUD guard disables this procedure in cloud deployments; only self-hosted instances are affected.

## Impact

Any authenticated user on a self-hosted multi-tenant Dokploy instance can read container resource metrics (CPU, memory, network I/O, block I/O) for any application by name, including containers belonging to other organizations and the Dokploy control-plane service itself.
